          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "In Ipswich, Queensland, estate litigation and **Family Provision Applications (FPAs)** are governed by the *Succession Act 1981 (Qld)*. Several established law firms with physical offices in Ipswich or the Greater Springfield area handle will disputes, testamentary capacity challenges, and estate litigation:\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Walker Pender Lawyers**\n* **Location:** Ipswich CBD\n* **Practice Focus:** Walker Pender is one of the longest-standing local firms in Ipswich. They have a dedicated will disputes team that represents clients contesting unfair wills, applicants bringing Family Provision claims, and executors needing to defend the estate against legal challenges.\n* **Key Areas:** Lack of provision for spouses/children/dependants, time-sensitive FPA filings, and negotiated settlements.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **McNamara Law**\n* **Location:** Ipswich (opposite the courts) & Springfield Central\n* **Practice Focus:** A large, historic regional firm with a dedicated wills, estates, and litigation team. They routinely appear in the Supreme Court for complex succession matters.\n* **Key Areas:** Family Provision Applications, solemn form proceedings (challenging will validity on grounds of mental capacity or undue influence), and removing executors who have breached their fiduciary duties.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Jacaranda Law**\n* **Location:** Ipswich\n* **Practice Focus:** A boutique firm with an exclusive focus on wills and estates law. Because they do not divide resources across other general practice areas, their practice concentrates heavily on estate planning, probate, and will dispute resolution.\n* **Key Areas:** Challenging will validity (undue influence, lack of capacity), contesting an unfair distribution (FPA), and advising executors defending estates.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Fallu McMillan Lawyers**\n* **Location:** Ipswich\n* **Practice Focus:** A prominent local Ipswich practice providing comprehensive estate and litigation services.\n* **Key Areas:** Estate litigation, defending estates against claims, advising family members who have been excluded or treated unfairly in a will, and resolving executor/beneficiary conflicts.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Keane Legal**\n* **Location:** Brookwater & Ipswich\n* **Practice Focus:** Servicing Ipswich and the Greater Springfield corridor, Keane Legal offers focused succession law services.\n* **Key Areas:** Family provision applications, testamentary capacity disputes, advising beneficiaries on statutory rights, and advising executors on their legal liabilities.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Turner Freeman Lawyers**\n* **Location:** 10 Churchill Street, Ipswich\n* **Practice Focus:** A large litigation firm with a local Ipswich branch. They maintain an active wills and estate litigation team and often offer \"no win, no fee\" arrangements for eligible estate contest cases.\n* **Key Areas:** Contested wills, large-scale estate disputes, and high-value Family Provision claims under Queensland law.\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Bennett Carroll Solicitors**\n* **Location:** Ipswich (plus Brisbane and Gold Coast)\n* **Practice Focus:** A Queensland-wide firm with an Ipswich presence handling will challenges and estate distribution actions.\n* **Key Areas:** Succession Act claims for spouses, children, and stepchildren, as well as formal challenges based on execution errors or undue influence.\n\n---\n\n### Key Statutory Time Limits in Queensland\nIf you are planning to bring an FPA in Ipswich, keep in mind the strict deadlines set by the *Succession Act 1981 (Qld)*:\n* **6 Months:** You must give written notice of your intention to claim to the estate executor within **6 months** of the deceased’s date of death.\n* **9 Months:** Formal court proceedings must be filed within **9 months** of the date of death.",
